WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Response validation is necessary even when the remote service is
authenticated and transport is encrypted.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:15.298
The response can still be malformed, oversized, stale, missing fields,
using an unexpected content type, or inconsistent with the business rules
of the application.

3
00:00:15.298 --> 00:00:24.949
The Python client should treat the response as input from another system
and validate it before using it to update records, make decisions, display
data, or trigger workflow.

4
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:28.757
Status codes and content types should be interpreted deliberately.

5
00:00:28.757 --> 00:00:31.252
A success status should not be the only check.

6
00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:33.549
The content type should match the parser.

7
00:00:33.549 --> 00:00:36.570
A JSON response should have expected fields and types.

8
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:43.004
XML, YAML, CSV, archive, or binary responses require additional caution
based on how they will be parsed and stored.

9
00:00:43.004 --> 00:00:50.160
Error responses should be handled safely without exposing remote details
or assuming the request can be retried automatically.

10
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.640
Response size limits and read timeouts protect availability.

11
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.892
A remote system may return far more data than expected, stream slowly, or
never finish sending.

12
00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:03.488
The client should know how much data is reasonable and how long it is
willing to wait.

13
00:01:03.488 --> 00:01:11.564
If a response is cached, the cache key, expiration, privacy level, and
invalidation behavior should match the data sensitivity and user context.

14
00:01:11.564 --> 00:01:15.175
Business-rule validation is as important as schema validation.

15
00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:24.038
A response may have the right fields but refer to the wrong tenant, an
unexpected account, an unauthorized state transition, or a value outside
the current user?

16
00:01:24.038 --> 00:01:25.088
s permission.

17
00:01:25.088 --> 00:01:29.487
Secure clients do not blindly trust remote systems to enforce every local
rule.

18
00:01:29.487 --> 00:01:32.442
They validate the response against the application?

19
00:01:32.442 --> 00:01:34.280
s own purpose before acting on it.
